                <p>There can only exist one primary key in a table. There can be more than one candidate key in relation out of which one can be chosen as the primary key. It is a unique key. It can identify only one record at a time. It cannot be null.</p>
                <HR>
                <li style="font-weight:600; list-style: none; font-size:20px">Super key</li>
                <p>The set of attributes that can uniquely identify a tuple is known as super key. A super key is a group of single or multiple keys that identifies rows in a column. It supports null value.</p>
                <HR>
                <li style="font-weight:600; list-style: none; font-size:20px">Alternate key</li>
                <p>The candidate key other than the primary key is called an alternate key. IN other words, all the keys which are not primary keys are called alternate keys. It is a secondary key. It contains two or more fields to identify two or more records. Those values are repeated.</p>
                <HR>
                <li style="font-weight:600; list-style: none; font-size:20px">Foreign key</li>
                <p>If an attribute can only take the values which are present as values of some other attribute, it will be a foreign key to the attribute to which it refers. The relation which is being referenced is called referenced relation and the corresponding attribute is called referenced attribute. It acts as a primary key in one table and it acts as secondary key in another table.</p>
                <HR>
                <li style="font-weight:600; list-style: none; font-size:20px">Composite key</li>
                <p>Sometimes, a table might not have a single column attribute that uniquely identifies all the records of a table. To uniquely identify rows of a table, a combination of two or more columns or attributes can be used. It acts as a primary key if there is no primary key in the table. Two or more attributes are used together to make a composite key.</p>
                    <HR>
